#original-page(595pt, 842pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(294.705pt, 804.24pt, "2 ")
  #line-at(80.88pt, 68.16pt, "The thermal management of high-power electronic devices has become increasingly critical as ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 95.76pt, "power densities continue to rise.")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 7.92pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(209.496pt, 91.68pt, "1")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(213.496pt, 95.76pt, " Silicon carbide (SiC) is a cornerstone material for such applications, ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 123.36pt, "yet its own heat dissipation capacity is often overwhelmed, leading to performance degradation and ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 150.96pt, "reliability concerns.")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 7.92pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(153.837pt, 146.88pt, "2, 3")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(166.998pt, 150.96pt, " Diamond, with the highest known room-temperature thermal conductivity ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 178.8pt, "(")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(60.876pt, 178.8pt, "~2000 W m")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8.4pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(122.329pt, 174.48pt, "!\"")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(133.929pt, 178.8pt, " K")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 8.4pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(144.593pt, 174.48pt, "!\"")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(156.194pt, 178.8pt, "), represents a compelling solution for integrated heat spreading. However, the ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 206.88pt, "practical integration of high-quality diamond with SiC remains challenging. Although the lattice ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 234.48pt, "mismatch between diamond and common hexagonal SiC polytypes is relatively small, the large ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 262.08pt, "disparity in thermal expansion coefficients generates substantial compressive stress during cooling ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 289.68pt, "from the growth temperature. As a result, directly grown diamond films commonly suffer from ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 317.28pt, "cracking, poor interfacial adhesion, and severe limitations in achievable thickness.")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 7.92pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(491.799pt, 313.2pt, "4-6")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(502.463pt, 317.28pt, " These ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 344.88pt, "constraints motivate the exploration of deposition techniques that balance film quality, scalability, ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 372.48pt, "and cost. ")
  #line-at(80.88pt, 400.08pt, "While microwave plasma chemical vapor deposition (MPCVD) produces the highest quality ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 427.68pt, "diamond films, its high cost and limitations in scaling for large-area substrates constrain its ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 455.28pt, "widespread industrial adoption.")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 7.92pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(209.559pt, 451.2pt, "7")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(213.559pt, 455.28pt, " In this context, hot filament chemical vapor deposition (HFCVD) ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 482.88pt, "emerges as a compelling, technologically viable alternative. HFCVD offers superior scalability, lower ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 510.48pt, "operational costs, and compatibility with non-planar geometries, making it a more practical route for ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 538.08pt, "implementing diamond thermal management in commercial devices.")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 7.92pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(381.951pt, 534pt, "8")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(385.951pt, 538.08pt, " The central challenge, therefore, ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 565.68pt, "shifts from merely growing diamond to engineering a cost-effective HFCVD diamond film with ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 593.28pt, "optimized thermal performance despite its inherent microstructural imperfections. ")
  #line-at(80.88pt, 620.88pt, "A key, yet often overlooked, aspect of polycrystalline diamond films is their pronounced ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 648.48pt, "structural gradation. HFCVD films typically nucleate with fine, defective crystallites and impurities, ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 676.08pt, "evolving towards larger, more thermally conductive grains at the surface.")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 7.92pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(444.38pt, 672pt, "9, 10")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(463.876pt, 676.08pt, " This intrinsic ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 703.68pt, "heterogeneity implies that the thermal conductivity is not a uniform bulk property but is depth-")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 731.04pt, "dependent.")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 7.92pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(109.192pt, 726.96pt, "11-13")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(127.856pt, 731.04pt, " A conventional, bulk-averaged thermal measurement obscures this critical detail and ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 758.64pt, "fails to provide the necessary insight for targeted process optimization. Understanding the depth-")
]

// arXiv source page 3
#original-page(595pt, 842pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(294.705pt, 804.24pt, "3 ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 68.16pt, "resolved thermal transport is paramount for answering fundamental questions: How quickly does the ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 95.76pt, "thermal conductivity improve from the defective nucleation region? What is the ultimate thermal ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 123.36pt, "performance of the higher-quality surface layer? The answers are essential for making informed ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 150.96pt, "trade-offs between film thickness, growth time, cost, and final thermal performance. ")
  #line-at(80.88pt, 178.56pt, "In this work, we address this critical knowledge gap by employing a sophisticated square-pulsed ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 206.16pt, "source (SPS) thermometry technique to directly measure the depth-dependent thermal conductivity ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 233.76pt, "of HFCVD diamond films grown on SiC. We correlate the thermal transport profile with meticulous ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 261.36pt, "microstructural characterization from electron backscatter diffraction (EBSD) and transmission ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 288.96pt, "electron microscopy (TEM). By fitting the frequency-dependent thermal data to a depth-resolved ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 316.56pt, "conductivity model, we quantitatively reconstruct the thermal conductivity as a function of film depth. ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 344.16pt, "Our findings reveal a significant gradient in thermal conduction, directly linking it to the ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 371.76pt, "microstructural evolution. This study provides not only a fundamental understanding of heat transport ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 399.36pt, "in graded polycrystalline films but also a practical framework for guiding the development of high-")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 426.96pt, "performance, cost-effective diamond thermal solutions for next-generation electronics. ")
  #line-at(80.88pt, 454.56pt, "The experimental details are briefly summarized as follows. Polycrystalline diamond films were ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 482.16pt, "deposited on 4H-SiC substrates using HFCVD. An optimized filament-substrate distance of 8 mm ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 509.76pt, "and a filament power of 900 W per tantalum filament were employed to minimize intrinsic stress and ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 537.36pt, "maximize crystalline quality. The optimization process, including SEM and Raman studies of the ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 564.96pt, "diamond films grown using different filament-substrate distances and filament powers, is described ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 592.56pt, "in detail in Supplementary Information Sec. S1. Stable filament carbonization was ensured through ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 620.16pt, "precise current control and a 1-hour pre-treatment step, producing a consistent gas-phase environment ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 647.76pt, "for diamond nucleation.  ")
  #line-at(80.88pt, 675.36pt, "To enhance diamond nucleation density and mitigate thermal expansion mismatch during high-")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 702.96pt, "temperature growth, a ~20 nm amorphous silicon nitride (SiN) interlayer was introduced as a ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 730.56pt, "compliant buffer layer between the diamond film and the SiC substrate. The SiN layer was deposited ")
  #line-at(56.88pt, 758.16pt, "using magnetron sputtering at 200°C and 1 Pa, conditions determined to yield a high N/Si ratio, ")
]

